30 cases, the same retrieved context for every arm, 2 runs each.

                   ceiling  correct  refused cut off    p50 out tokens reasoning
  gpt-5.4-mini         250   19/30     5/12        0   0.9s        616         0
                       250   22/30     6/12        0   0.9s        554         0
  gpt-5.5              250   20/30     8/12        2   2.6s      1,743     1,189
                       250   20/30     8/12        2   2.8s      1,767     1,194
  gpt-5.5            2,000   18/30     6/12        0   2.1s      1,781     1,093
                     2,000   20/30     8/12        0   2.9s      1,844     1,216

  (no rates set, so this table reports tokens and stays silent about
   dollars — Appendix C says where to find current prices)

Read the cut-off column first. At a 250 token ceiling the smart model stopped at
'length' 4 times across 2 runs, 4 of them with no visible text at all: its
reasoning tokens count against the same ceiling as its answer. Scored as it
stands, that arm partly measures max_tokens rather than the model.

Then the runs. The same arm scored up to 3 cases apart from one run to the next.
A gap between models smaller than that is not a finding.

This is the table the gateway exists to make possible, and it is worth being
precise about what it does and does not say.

It says how these arms scored on this suite, at these latencies, for these token
counts. That is a fact about Meridian's questions and Meridian's documents,
measured on one afternoon.

It does not say which model is better. Nothing does. There is no such number,
and the published leaderboards answer a question nobody in your building is
asking — Chapter 21 is about why your own cases are the only ones
that transfer.

Three things to check before trusting any row of this table:

  the context was held fixed   both models saw identical passages, fetched
                               once. Re-running retrieval per model measures
                               retrieval variance and calls it a model
                               difference
  the ceiling was fair         one output ceiling is not one condition when
                               one model reasons before it answers
  the interval is wide         §21.6's arithmetic applies here unchanged; on
                               thirty cases a gap of a few cases is nothing

And a provider comparison is not only a quality comparison. This chapter's last
section is about the fact that compliance, data residency and contractual
terms routinely decide this before capability gets a vote — and they are much
harder to change later than a model name in a config file.
